f 1069 "q-96821" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134550 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134550 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ is the standard Hermitian inner product over $C,$ find $⟨a,\\,b-(1+3\\text{i})c⟩$ if \n\n$\\,$ $⟨b,a⟩=1+\\text{i}\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,c⟩=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134550 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8f2e5445-afeb-5118-844a-129faadbb234" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134550 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties. \n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nFirst, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ is antilinear in the second argument:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨a,\\,b-(1+3\\text{i})c⟩ & =⟨a,b⟩-⟨a,(1+3\\text{i})c⟩ \\\\  & =⟨a,b⟩-(\\overset{―}{1+3\\text{i}})⟨a,c⟩ \\\\  & =⟨a,b⟩-(1-3\\text{i})⟨a,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨a,b⟩-(1-3\\text{i})⟨a,c⟩ & =\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}-(1-3\\text{i})⟨a,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the values for $⟨b,a⟩$ and $⟨a,c⟩:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}-(1-3\\text{i})⟨a,c⟩ & =\\overset{―}{1+\\text{i}}-(1-3\\text{i})\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =1-\\text{i}-2+6\\text{i} \\\\  & =-1+5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134636 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134637 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134636 #edb/ref 1061 20193 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134636 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5959b5ca-9405-5f0f-b4d5-5a7a8e442cfc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134636 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134637 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134804 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134637 #edb/ref 1061 20194 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134637 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1f2f6fb1-f106-52c7-b55a-201253d4ed07" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134637 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133765 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134638 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134805 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134638 #edb/ref 1061 20196 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134638 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f393944b-d78a-5d8a-aa1a-c7c5133f9717" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134638 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133766 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134804 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134638 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134804 #edb/ref 1061 31867 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134804 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f16cfe03-2cd2-5567-81c0-5f200f0b4b5f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134804 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135100 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134805 #edb/ref 1061 31868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134805 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "418336f6-44aa-5629-a383-cc4e0867f9c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134805 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133865 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135034 #edb/ref 1064 "The standard inner product of $\\Bbb R^n,$ i.e. the dot product, does not meet the criteria of an inner product on $\\Bbb C^n.$\n\nFor example, if $\\langle \\,\\cdot\\,, \\,\\cdot\\, \\rangle$ is a valid inner product on $\\Bbb C^n,$ then for any vector $\\mathbf a \\in \\Bbb C^n,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\langle\\mathbf a, \\, \\mathbf a\\rangle \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nBut for many vectors in $\\Bbb C^n,$ such as $[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ in $\\Bbb C^2,$ the dot product fails to satisfy this criterion:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & ={\\text{i}}^{2}+{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-1 \\\\  & <0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThankfully, there is an elegant way to generalize the dot product to satisfy the desired criterion on $\\Bbb C^n.$ We can just take the complex conjugate of each element in the right vector before carrying out the dot product.\n\nMore formally, for two vectors $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf y\\in \\mathbb C^n$ given by\n\n![](images/26/26b805ac1354eab1901a0874f66882bc1ec07dc3351c95ad2cf9b16aaf59d2fb.svg)\n\nwe define the **standard Hermitian inner product** as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & ={x}_{1}\\cdot \\overset{―}{{y}_{1}}+{x}_{2}\\cdot \\overset{―}{{y}_{2}}+⋯+{x}_{n}\\cdot \\overset{―}{{y}_{n}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\cdot \\overset{―}{{y}_{i}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overline{y_i}$ is the complex conjugate of $y_i.$\n\nUsing the standard Hermitian inner product instead of the dot product, our desired criterion is satisfied:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]⟩ & =\\text{i}\\cdot \\overset{―}{\\,\\text{i}\\,}+0\\cdot \\overset{―}{\\,0\\,} \\\\  & =\\text{i}\\cdot (-\\text{i})+0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =1+0 \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & \\ge 0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt can be verified that the Hermitian inner product satisfies *all* the criteria of an inner product on $\\Bbb C^n.$\n\nFurthermore, since the complex conjugate leaves real numbers unchanged, the Hermitian inner product simplifies to the dot product on $\\Bbb R^n.$ In other words, the dot product is just a special case of the Hermitian inner product!" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135034 #edb/ref 1065 3605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135034 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ca70c748-2628-5d2d-b0dc-ab7a0675355a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135034 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135100 #edb/ref 1064 "A Hermitian inner product over $\\mathbb{C}$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is **linear in the first argument.** For any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$ and any $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{C},$ we have \n\n$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle = \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle + \\langle \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle \\quad$ and $\\quad \\langle \\alpha \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\alpha \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle.$\n\n- It is **antilinear in the second argument.** For any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$ and any $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{C},$ we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} + \\mathbf{c} \\rangle = \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle + \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle \\quad$ and $\\quad \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\alpha\\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\overline{\\alpha} \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle.$\n\n- It has **conjugate symmetry.** For any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n,$ we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\overline{\\langle \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle}.$\n\n- It is **positive-definite.** For any $\\mathbf{a}\\in \\mathbb{C}^n,$ we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle$ is a non-negative real number, and $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle = 0$ if and only if $\\mathbf{a} = \\mathbf{0}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135100 #edb/ref 1065 5088 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135100 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "44cf93d3-a069-54ee-bfbf-93d36fd338cb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135100 #edb/ref 1067 "Properties of the Inner Product Over the Complex Numbers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269394 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269394 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269394 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0e483cc2-b46f-5512-a9a7-0f10ea8a953d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269869 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269869 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269869 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4288a7d7-b334-57bd-981d-9101054485a0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269954 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269954 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269954 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4bf5a7d9-2cd0-5edb-8159-5892ad0bc218" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270242 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270242 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270242 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6d857f4b-9335-543f-9a26-45ba2569561b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271085 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271085 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271085 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "cf364781-4d57-5ea3-a715-fb67ec1553ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271694 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186269394 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271694 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186269869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271694 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186269954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271694 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186270242 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271694 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186271085 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271694 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271694 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271694 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186270242 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271694 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "672798eb-6f95-52b3-882f-b5bf772f3083" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272227 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271694 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272227 #edb/ref 1069 "q-141160" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272227 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272227 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $⟨ia,b⟩=i⟨a,b⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $⟨a,\\overset{―}{\\alpha }b⟩=⟨\\alpha a,b⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C$\n- $⟨ia,a⟩\\in R$ for any $a\\in {C}^{n}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272227 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "396a82c3-ba69-5d62-8d39-94fffd303517" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272227 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true.  Indeed, the inner product in ${C}^{n}$ is linear in the first argument. So, a constant multiple from the first argument can be factored out.\n\n- Statement II is true. By the antilinearity of a complex inner product in the second argument and linearity in the first argument, we have \n\n$$\n⟨a,\\overset{―}{\\alpha }b⟩=\\overset{―}{(\\overset{―}{\\alpha })}⟨a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩=⟨\\alpha a,b⟩.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that $⟨a,a⟩\\in R$ for any $a\\in {C}^{n}.$ Therefore, if $a\\ne 0,$ then\n\n$$\n⟨ia,a⟩=i⟨a,a⟩\\notin R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357872 #edb/ref 1080 "5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357872 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 1759218