0009 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360009 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360009 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186359973 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360009 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "05a26644-5b9a-583b-8d85-eae43b25ea9a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360116 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186358030 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360116 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186358369 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360116 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186359313 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360116 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186359611 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360116 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186359861 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360116 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360116 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360116 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186358030 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360116 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "4ef80d06-a51d-581a-a204-f6dcc0201009" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360273 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186357872 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360273 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186357876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360273 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186359435 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360273 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186359541 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360273 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186359809 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360273 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360273 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360273 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186359541 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360273 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a14982e8-8062-5f22-9abd-32b12535e617" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360767 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360273 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360767 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97193" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360767 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360767 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ is the standard Hermitian inner product over $C,$ find $⟨a,\\,(4-\\text{i})b+c⟩$ if  \n\n$\\,$ $⟨b,a⟩=-1-\\text{i}\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,c⟩=5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360767 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "34140471-4f5c-5a2d-9e46-e76840a3d73f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360767 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties. \n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nFirst, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ is antilinear in the second argument:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨a,\\,(4-\\text{i})b+c⟩ & =⟨a,(4-\\text{i})b⟩+⟨a,c⟩ \\\\  & =(\\overset{―}{4-\\text{i}})⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩ \\\\  & =(4+\\text{i})⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4+\\text{i})⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c\\text{ }⟩ & =(4+\\text{i})\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}+⟨a,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the values for $⟨b,a⟩$ and $⟨a,c⟩:$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4+\\text{i})\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}+⟨a,c⟩ & =(4+\\text{i})\\cdot (\\overset{―}{-1-\\text{i}})+5 \\\\  & =(4+\\text{i})\\cdot (-1+\\text{i})+5 \\\\  & =-5+3\\text{i}+5 \\\\  & =3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360774 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360009 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360774 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97258" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360774 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360774 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $⟨a,a⟩\\in R$ for any $a\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C$\n- $⟨a,b⟩=⟨b,a⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360774 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2fac0a4e-d3e8-507c-b2ff-dcb288cf0e43" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360774 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$  $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number.\n\n- Statement II is false. To factor out a constant multiple from the second argument, we must change it to its complex conjugate.\n\n- Statement III is false. The inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360783 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360116 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360783 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97296" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360783 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360783 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨a,b⟩}$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $i⟨a,b⟩=⟨a,-ib⟩$ for any  $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- There is vector $a\\in {C}^{n}$ such that  $⟨a,a⟩<0$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360783 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "354bca31-ea8a-5e1d-a896-d3e34e677d11" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360783 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. A complex number is equal to its conjugate if and only if it is a real number. The inner product over $C$  is a complex number in general.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, by the antilinearity of a complex inner product in the second argument, we have \n\n$$\n⟨a,-ib⟩=\\overset{―}{(-i)}⟨a,b⟩=i⟨a,b⟩.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false.  The inner product over $C$ is positive-definite. So, for any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩\\ge 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382621 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186382622 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382621 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186382623 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382621 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186382624 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382621 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186382625 #